  "slug": "neural-circuit-control-109b",
  "summary": "Recent connectome and in vivo recordings resolve specific circuit elements underlying visual computations and the behaviors they drive.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the resolution of specific circuit elements that implement a sensory computation and map it onto behavior. In the fly, connectome analysis identifies a recurrent LC14 circuit that computes binocular distance and shapes courtship and visual responses, while a single pair of descending neurons, DNa10, controls flight altitude by modulating wing kinematics and lift. In mouse visual cortex, dual calcium imaging separates VIP and NDNF interneurons, which carry distinct behavioral correlates: VIP tracks pupil diameter, and the two populations implement dissociable inhibitory motifs for state-dependent gain control. In Xenopus tadpoles, tegmental direction selectivity is present early and remains stable, whereas tectal output selectivity sharpens over development. What is new is the assignment of computations to identified cell types and connections rather than to whole regions, a level of resolution common to these studies despite differing species and methods.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
